Prove the product of two primitive polynomials in a UFD R[x] is primitive. Let f and g be primitive in R[x], meaning their content (the greatest common divisor of coefficients) is a unit in R. By Gauss's Lemma, for any prime ideal (p) in R (a UFD), if p divides all coefficients of fg, then (p) divides all coefficients of f or all coefficients of g. Since f and g are primitive, no such prime p exists, so (fg) contains no non-unit ideals consistent with primitivity, implying content(fg) = 1 (a unit in R). Thus, fg is primitive.

Show that coprime polynomials f and g in R[x] remain coprime in F[x], the field of fractions. Suppose fh + gk = 1 for some h, k ∈ R[x] (since gcd(f,g) = 1 in R[x]). Let F = Frac(R) and embed R[x] into F[x] via the ring homomorphism π (extending R → F). Then π(fh + gk) = π(f)π(h) + π(g)π(k) = 1 in F[x], proving fπ(h) + gπ(k) = 1 over F[x]. Hence, f and g are coprime in F[x].

